{"from": "site:ginosar-plain-roman-town", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-08-10-dalmanutha", "grade": "D", "note": "Ken Dark's 2013 surface survey reported 'a possible location of Dalmanutha in the Ginosar Plain' - a previously unrecognised Roman-era ruined town on the north-west lakeshore. A candidate, not a confirmed identification; the existence of any town named Dalmanutha is itself disputed."}
{"from": "site:ginosar-plain-roman-town", "type": "attests", "to": "place:dalmanutha", "grade": "D", "note": "The same candidate: a lakeshore town of the Roman period near where a place named Dalmanutha would sit, but 'possible' only, and Bethsaida's own experience shows how contested such identifications stay."}
{"from": "site:ginosar-plain-roman-town", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:mk-08-10-dalmanutha", "note": "The fetched article records Joel L. Watts disputing 'that there was ever a town called Dalmanutha', and Mark 8:10 itself reads Dalmanutha only in most manuscripts - Theta, f1, f13 and 565 read Magdala/Mageda, aligning Mark with Matthew's Magdala (recorded in the passage's variants)."}
{"from": "artifact:galilee-boat", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-08-10-dalmanutha", "grade": "C", "note": "The 1st-century fishing boat, found in 1986 on the north-west shore - the working lakeshore economy of the region Dalmanutha's boat trip ended in; Dark placed the boat's find spot on the candidate town's stretch of lakeshore. Context, not the place name."}
{"from": "site:et-tell", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-08-22-blind-man-bethsaida", "grade": "D", "note": "Et-Tell was 'inhabited on a lesser scale during the first centuries BCE and CE and sometimes identified with the town of Bethsaida'; the identification is disputed and the site lies about 2 km from the lakeshore (reused from ch 11; the dispute is recorded on the edge)."}
{"from": "site:el-araj", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-08-22-blind-man-bethsaida", "grade": "D", "note": "The 2017 Roman bathhouse and Notley's 'Roman village... pottery, coins, also stone vessels, which are typical of first-century Jewish life' support the identification, which its own excavators call a suggestion; Bethsaida 'has not been positively identified' (reused from ch 11)."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-eduyot-8-7", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-08-28-identifications", "grade": "C", "note": "Mishnah Eduyot 8:7 cites the expectation 'Behold I send to you Elijah the prophet' - the living Elijah-expectation behind the 'some say Elijah' answer (reused from ch 16). Redacted c. 200 CE; it attests the expectation, not the crowd's answer."}
{"from": "text:psalms-of-solomon-17", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-08-29-you-are-the-christ", "grade": "C", "note": "'Raise up unto them their king, the son of David' - a first-century BCE Jewish psalm expecting a Davidic messiah: the currency of the messianic title 'Christ' that Peter uses. It attests the expectation behind the title, not that Peter spoke these words (reused from ch 15)."}
{"from": "text:tacitus-histories-4-81", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-08-23-spittle-two-stage", "grade": "C", "note": "Tacitus, Hist. 4.81: the Alexandrian blind man begged Vespasian 'to moisten his cheeks and eye-balls with his spittle' - spittle as a healing agent for eyes in the period. A parallel from elsewhere in the empire; it attests the custom, not this healing (reused from ch 07)."}
